.TH LASTLOGIN 1
.SH NAME
lastlogin \- tell when you last logged in
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lastlogin
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lastlogin
prints the time and the terminal of the login before this one:
.PP
.nf
	Last login: Sun Oct 11 13:50:12 on tty02
.fi
.PP
and prints nothing the first time. XENIX's login does not say this
itself, so the start-up files that mkuser gives new accounts, .login
for the C shell and .profile for the Bourne shell, run lastlogin. An
account made before can add
.B /usr/bin/lastlogin
to its own.
.PP
lastlogin finds the login it was started from in /etc/utmp, as the
entry of the shell that ran it, and keeps it, with the one before, in
/usr/adm/lastlog. Run again in the same login, it prints the same
line. Run where there is no login shell above it, it only prints.
.SH FILES
.TP 20
/usr/adm/lastlog
each user's last two logins, at the user id times 24 bytes
.TP
/etc/utmp
who is logged in now
.SH SEE ALSO
login(1), who(1), finger(1)
.SH HISTORY
4.2BSD's login printed the time and the terminal of the last login,
kept in /usr/adm/lastlog; lastlogin does the same for XENIX's.
.SH AUTHORS
lastlogin was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH BUGS
Logins whose start-up files do not run lastlogin are not kept, and
installing the TOOLS package again starts /usr/adm/lastlog over, empty.
.PP
XENIX's own /etc/wtmp is no help here: it was found holding only the
logins since the machine last started.
